1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,820
The next set of disorders that we're going to cover are those that can lead to cirrhosis.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,580
Cirrhosis is end-stage liver damage, and it's characterized by disruption of the normal hepatic parenchyma

3
00:00:09,580 --> 00:00:16,440
by these broad bands of fibrosis, and in between the broad bands of fibrosis are regenerative nodules of hepatocytes.

4
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:22,140
Here's a picture of cirrhosis, and notice that you've got all these little circles of nodular tissue,

5
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:26,520
and then you can see in between them, these are separated by bands of fibrosis.

6
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:30,300
And normally, remember that the surface of the liver should be totally smooth,

7
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:36,660
and so you can clearly see that there's this nodular disruption of the surface of the liver indicative of cirrhosis.

8
00:00:37,220 --> 00:00:43,140
Were you to look at this on microscopic image, what you would see is broad bands of fibrosis, as I'm highlighting here,

9
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:46,900
which are separating nodules of liver parenchyma.

10
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:49,420
Again, this is the hallmark of cirrhosis.

11
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:56,400
And one of the things that's high yield concerning cirrhosis is that the fibrosis is mediated by the stellate cell,

12
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:59,660
and so this is important because the board examiners will go after it.

13
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:05,180
The stellate cell can actually secrete TGF-beta, and this is actually the key mediator of that fibrosis.

14
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:11,500
Recall that the stellate cell lies beneath the endothelial cells that line the sinusoids of the hepatic plates.

15
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:15,800
When you think about the clinical features of cirrhosis, they're all based on damage to the liver.

16
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:20,760
For example, one category of clinical findings will be based on portal hypertension.

17
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:27,220
Because there will be portal hypertension, all of the organs that are draining through the portal system will have an increased hydrostatic pressure.

18
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:30,740
This will lead to ascites, which is fluid in the peritoneal cavity.

19
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:35,740
It will lead to enlargement of the spleen, which is called congestive splenomegaly.

20
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:40,660
And that enlarged spleen can actually become hyperactive and begin to consume red cells or platelets,

21
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:46,640
and that would be called hypersplenism, that is the consumption of red cells and platelets by an enlarged spleen.

22
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:54,180
In addition, we can see portal systemic shunts, and some examples include esophageal varices, hemorrhoids, and caplet medusae.

23
00:01:54,560 --> 00:01:58,740
All of these are examples of portal systemic shunts because you can't get the blood through the liver.

24
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:01,220
And a final complication is hepatorenal syndrome.

25
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:05,880
This is a rapidly developing renal failure in the context or in the face of cirrhosis.

26
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:13,400
Of course, the liver plays an important role in detoxifying the blood, and if we lose liver function, that role will be decreased.

27
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:18,780
This will result in mental status changes associated with asterixis and eventually leading to coma.

28
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:25,200
These mental status changes would be due to increased serum ammonia, and this would be reversible because it is metabolic.

29
00:02:25,940 --> 00:02:29,720
In addition, the liver plays an important role in removing estrogen from the blood,

30
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:34,800
and so if the liver is not able to do so, we will get signs and symptoms of excess estrogen.

31
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:39,800
For example, gynecomastia, spider angiometa, and palmar erythema.

32
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:43,600
All of these are signs and symptoms of excess estrogen.

33
00:02:44,020 --> 00:02:52,820
And finally, we could get jaundice in the context of cirrhosis because of the fact that the liver hepatocytes play an important role in conjugating bilirubin.

34
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:58,240
Another important manifestation of cirrhosis would arise because of decreased protein synthesis.

35
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:04,720
For example, the liver plays an important role in making albumin, but if the liver is not functioning, there would be hypoalbuminemia.

36
00:03:05,320 --> 00:03:09,660
Of course, this would also decrease the oncotic pressure of the blood, further leading to edema.

37
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:17,920
In addition, the liver makes the vast majority of coagulation factors and also plays an important role in activating vitamin K via epoxide reductase,

38
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:20,240
and so therefore the patients can develop a coagulopathy.

39
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:25,580
Now, it's important to note that with this coagulopathy, both the PT and the PTT would rise.

40
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:28,600
However, the PT is used to follow the degree of deficiency.

41
00:03:29,120 --> 00:03:32,380
And the way I remember this is that it's very similar to the warfarin effect.

42
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:38,620
Of course, warfarin blocks epoxide reductase, and the PT is used to follow the degree of warfarin effect.

43
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:42,680
And in the same way in coagulopathy, you lose epoxide reductase,

44
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:49,500
and therefore PT is used to follow the effect of cirrhosis on the status of the coagulation system.

45
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:54,580
One of the mechanisms by which cirrhosis can develop is alcohol-related liver disease.

46
00:03:55,360 --> 00:04:00,720
Now, alcohol-related liver disease can actually present with three classic patterns of injury.

47
00:04:01,160 --> 00:04:05,040
In each case, there is damage of the hepatic parenchyma due to consumption of alcohol.

48
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:08,720
The first change that we can see with alcohol is called fatty liver.

49
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:14,760
Here, we get accumulation of fat within the hepatocytes, which then results in a heavy, greasy liver.

50
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:18,240
This is reversible and resolves with abstinence.

51
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:20,860
Here's an image of fatty change within the liver.

52
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:25,400
Notice all these circular clear spaces indicating that there is fat in the cells.

53
00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:30,900
Here's a gross image of a liver with fatty change, and it appears large and slightly yellow,

54
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:33,860
and has a greasy or shiny appearance.

55
00:04:34,520 --> 00:04:38,780
Another pathology that can arise with alcohol is called alcoholic hepatitis.

56
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:45,020
This is direct chemical injury to the hepatocytes by alcohol, and it generally is seen with binge drinking.

57
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:51,480
Now, the key mediator here is acetylaldehyde, which is a derivative of alcohol, and that's what then damages the hepatocytes.

58
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:56,100
The key finding in alcoholic hepatitis will be swelling or ballooning of the hepatocytes.

59
00:04:56,240 --> 00:05:00,560
Note the enlargement and the ballooning of these cells with necrosis and acute inflammation,

60
00:05:00,980 --> 00:05:03,980
and one highly characteristic feature called the malary body.

61
00:05:03,980 --> 00:05:08,520
And here I'm circling for you the multiple malary bodies seen in these hepatocytes.

62
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:16,600
It's high yield to note, for the purposes of board examination, that the malary bodies are damaged intermediate filaments within the hepatocytes.

63
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:24,040
Board examiners will ask you what the malary bodies contain, and again, those are damaged intermediate filaments within the hepatocytes.

64
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:30,140
The presentation of alcoholic hepatitis is painful hepatomegaly with elevated liver enzymes.

65
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:35,380
However, one very high yield point to note is that the AST will be greater than the ALT.

66
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:41,060
Now, this pattern is a little different than what we would expect to see in most other causes of liver damage.

67
00:05:41,440 --> 00:05:47,360
And the reason is that the AST is actually located in the mitochondria, and alcohol is a mitochondrial poison.

68
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:51,820
And so when we damage the mitochondria, we preferentially get an increase in the AST.

69
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:56,860
And again, that's a fairly characteristic marker of alcohol-related damage of the liver.

70
00:05:57,260 --> 00:05:59,360
Alcoholic hepatitis can result in death.

71
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:04,220
The third possible pattern of damage to the liver in the face of alcohol is cirrhosis.

72
00:06:04,700 --> 00:06:10,540
This is a complication of chronic alcohol-induced liver damage, and it's seen in about 10 to 20% of alcoholics.

73
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:15,620
Of course, patients with cirrhosis would present with the signs and symptoms that I've covered just a little while ago.

74
00:06:15,620 --> 00:06:21,380
Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease is when you get fatty change, hepatitis, and or cirrhosis.

75
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:23,740
So these are the three key changes we saw with alcohol.

76
00:06:24,220 --> 00:06:27,080
However, these occur without a known exposure to alcohol.

77
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:31,380
This is generally associated with obesity, and it's a diagnosis of exclusion.

78
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:34,740
That is that you sort of make the assumption that alcohol must be involved

79
00:06:34,740 --> 00:06:38,580
until you've absolutely proved that the patient is not abusing alcohol.

80
00:06:39,020 --> 00:06:43,120
One very helpful finding here would be that the ALT would be greater than the AST.

81
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:46,720
And again, that's because in this particular case, alcohol is not involved.

82
00:06:47,060 --> 00:06:50,900
And you're well aware, as I just mentioned, that the AST is a mitochondrial enzyme

83
00:06:50,900 --> 00:06:54,540
and is preferentially increased when there is alcohol-based damage.

84
00:06:55,920 --> 00:06:58,520
Hemochromatosis is another disorder that can lead to cirrhosis.

85
00:06:59,060 --> 00:07:04,700
And in hemochromatosis, we get excess body iron that deposits in tissues and then damages organs.

86
00:07:05,060 --> 00:07:07,540
The deposition in tissues is called hemociderosis,

87
00:07:07,820 --> 00:07:11,540
and when it actually damages the tissues, we call that hemochromatosis.

88
00:07:11,540 --> 00:07:16,680
Now, very high yield to note, the damage is actually caused by the generation of free radicals.

89
00:07:17,020 --> 00:07:22,860
Recall that iron has the ability, via the Fenton reaction, to cause the formation of free radicals.

90
00:07:23,100 --> 00:07:27,120
And so when you get excess iron within the tissues, you'll get generation of free radicals,

91
00:07:27,140 --> 00:07:29,920
and that's what mediates the damage in this particular disorder.

92
00:07:30,460 --> 00:07:34,200
Now, it's important to note that hemochromatosis can be primary or secondary.

93
00:07:34,860 --> 00:07:38,400
Let's go back and review a little bit of biochemistry in order to understand this distinction.

94
00:07:38,400 --> 00:07:44,380
Recall that the enterocyte in the duodenum takes up iron from the lumen of the gut.

95
00:07:44,880 --> 00:07:49,740
Now, the enterocyte will basically take up almost all the iron that is present within the gut.

96
00:07:50,060 --> 00:07:54,120
However, the enterocyte will hold on to it and will not pass it off into the blood

97
00:07:54,120 --> 00:07:56,480
unless there is a need within the blood.

98
00:07:56,760 --> 00:08:01,560
So basically, the enterocyte has the ability to detect whether or not iron is necessary

99
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:06,000
and will only pass the iron off into the blood when iron is required.

100
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:10,980
Now, that's important because this is the key regulatory step in the human body for iron.

101
00:08:11,300 --> 00:08:14,240
Remember that the human body has no way of getting rid of iron.

102
00:08:14,700 --> 00:08:17,200
Yes, the female can lose a little bit in her menstrual cycle,

103
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:20,380
and yes, we do lose a little bit by the sloughing of our skin.

104
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:24,120
However, for all practical purposes, there is no real way to get rid of iron.

105
00:08:24,540 --> 00:08:27,820
And so the regulatory role of the enterocyte is extremely important.

106
00:08:28,260 --> 00:08:32,000
Now, what happens in primary hemochromatosis is that dysregulation gets disrupted

107
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,500
so that whatever comes into the enterocyte goes directly into the blood.

108
00:08:35,940 --> 00:08:38,700
That, of course, is going to result in a massive iron overload,

109
00:08:39,040 --> 00:08:41,860
which is going to deposit in tissues as hemosiderosis

110
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:45,840
and eventually will damage the tissues, resulting in hemochromatosis.

111
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:49,860
And so this is the basic pathogenesis of primary hemochromatosis.

112
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:52,660
Now, it's important to note that in primary hemochromatosis,

113
00:08:52,820 --> 00:08:55,620
the reason why this enterocyte dysregulation occurs

114
00:08:55,620 --> 00:08:59,300
is because you have mutations in a gene called the HFE gene.

115
00:08:59,300 --> 00:09:02,980
And the most common mutation is called C282Y.

116
00:09:03,640 --> 00:09:07,220
Another way by which we can get hemochromatosis is secondary hemochromatosis.

117
00:09:07,640 --> 00:09:12,200
In this particular case, hemochromatosis arises as a complication of transfusions.

118
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:14,400
Remember that every time we give a transfusion,

119
00:09:14,500 --> 00:09:17,820
the red blood cells are only going to last for a maximum 120 days.

120
00:09:18,220 --> 00:09:19,900
Now, those red blood cells will be turned over,

121
00:09:20,200 --> 00:09:23,680
and when the hemoglobin in the cells is turned over, the iron will be recycled.

122
00:09:24,320 --> 00:09:27,760
And then there is no way to get rid of that iron, as I've just mentioned earlier.

123
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:30,800
So every bag of blood is basically a bag of iron.

124
00:09:31,200 --> 00:09:35,560
Eventually, all of that iron will build up in the tissues, causing hemochromatosis,

125
00:09:36,120 --> 00:09:39,860
and eventually the organs will get damaged, resulting in hemochromatosis.

126
00:09:40,180 --> 00:09:42,820
This disorder usually presents in late adulthood

127
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:46,000
because it takes time for the iron to build up and damage the tissues.

128
00:09:46,380 --> 00:09:50,840
And the classic triad is cirrhosis, secondary diabetes, and bronze skin.

129
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:54,160
Now, basically what's happening here is that as iron deposits in these tissues,

130
00:09:54,160 --> 00:09:57,340
you get end-stage damage, which then destroys the tissues.

131
00:09:57,720 --> 00:10:00,120
So, again, cirrhosis would be end-stage liver damage.

132
00:10:00,440 --> 00:10:03,620
You get damage to the islets, resulting in secondary diabetes mellitus.

133
00:10:03,960 --> 00:10:08,600
This is also called bronze diabetes because the pancreas has a bronze color on gross exam.

134
00:10:08,920 --> 00:10:12,000
And then you get bronzing of the skin because iron can deposit in the skin.

135
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:16,220
Additional findings are cardiac arrhythmia, if the iron deposits in the heart,

136
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:20,180
and gonadal dysfunction because iron can also deposit within the testicle,

137
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:22,100
resulting in testicular atrophy.

138
00:10:22,100 --> 00:10:26,000
Now, the lab findings are going to be based on an iron-overloaded state.

139
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:29,880
So, because the tissues are loaded with iron and you have tremendous iron stores,

140
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:31,140
the ferritin will be high.

141
00:10:31,460 --> 00:10:34,680
Of course, any time the ferritin is high, the TIBC is also low.

142
00:10:35,140 --> 00:10:37,260
Much of that iron will actually leak out into the blood,

143
00:10:37,520 --> 00:10:41,260
giving you a high serum iron with a high percent saturation.

144
00:10:41,900 --> 00:10:44,820
And so these are the key lab findings in hemochromatosis.

145
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:49,460
When you suspect hemochromatosis, you'll perform a biopsy, usually of the liver,

146
00:10:49,460 --> 00:10:52,080
and you'll see a brown pigment within the hepatocytes.

147
00:10:52,520 --> 00:10:55,440
Now, again, remember that when you have a brown pigment in the hepatocytes,

148
00:10:55,500 --> 00:10:56,940
there's a couple things that it could be.

149
00:10:57,260 --> 00:10:59,580
For example, the brown pigment could be lipofuscin,

150
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:02,880
which is a wear-and-tear pigment that would be present within the liver cells.

151
00:11:03,220 --> 00:11:05,360
It could also be iron from hemochromatosis.

152
00:11:05,780 --> 00:11:10,340
And so one way by which we can make the distinction is to do a special stain called a Prussian blue stain.

153
00:11:10,740 --> 00:11:14,900
The Prussian blue stain will turn iron blue but will not mark the lipofuscin,

154
00:11:14,900 --> 00:11:16,960
i.e. the lipofuscin will remain brown.

155
00:11:17,440 --> 00:11:18,900
Here's a picture of hemochromatosis,

156
00:11:19,060 --> 00:11:22,660
and you can clearly see the brown pigment piling up within the hepatocytes.

157
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:25,360
Now, again, in order to prove that this brown pigment is iron,

158
00:11:25,460 --> 00:11:28,340
we can do a Prussian blue stain, and the iron would turn blue.

159
00:11:28,560 --> 00:11:31,840
Just for comparative purposes, and because examiners like to go after it,

160
00:11:32,080 --> 00:11:35,340
this is a picture of lipofuscin within the hepatocytes.

161
00:11:35,660 --> 00:11:37,500
Notice that it is also a brown pigment,

162
00:11:37,880 --> 00:11:43,820
and this is a wear-and-tear pigment that is derived from the breakdown of peroxidized lipids.

163
00:11:43,820 --> 00:11:49,400
This pigment piles up in the lysosomes and is just basically a sign that the cells have aged.

164
00:11:49,660 --> 00:11:51,740
It's sort of like the gray hair of the cells,

165
00:11:51,860 --> 00:11:54,760
so that when you see lipofuscin, you know that the cell is old

166
00:11:54,760 --> 00:11:56,880
and has been around for a very long period of time.

167
00:11:57,520 --> 00:12:00,460
Patients who have hemochromatosis can be treated by phlebotomy.

168
00:12:00,820 --> 00:12:02,960
The idea here is to remove red cells,

169
00:12:03,060 --> 00:12:06,120
and of course red cells contain iron, which would decrease the iron load.

170
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:09,760
Also important to note, there's an increased risk of hepatocellular carcinoma.

171
00:12:09,860 --> 00:12:13,120
Again, that's because the iron will generate all these free radicals,

172
00:12:13,820 --> 00:12:17,600
can damage DNA, which would then increase the risk of potential mutations

173
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:20,580
to form hepatocellular or liver carcinoma.

174
00:12:21,460 --> 00:12:25,060
Wilson's disease is another disorder that can result in cirrhosis.

175
00:12:25,620 --> 00:12:31,240
It's an autosomal recessive defect in the ATP-mediated hepatocyte copper transport system.

176
00:12:31,660 --> 00:12:34,580
This results in an inability to put copper into bile,

177
00:12:35,060 --> 00:12:39,280
and it also results in an inability to incorporate copper into ceruloplasmin,

178
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:42,680
which is the key molecule that carries copper around in the blood.

179
00:12:43,340 --> 00:12:46,060
As a result, because you can't put copper into the bile,

180
00:12:46,220 --> 00:12:47,920
copper will build up in the hepatocytes.

181
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:51,440
It will then leak into the serum and eventually deposit in tissues.

182
00:12:51,860 --> 00:12:55,420
Now copper, just like iron, has the ability to induce free radical damage,

183
00:12:55,780 --> 00:12:58,020
and so hydroxyl free radicals will be produced,

184
00:12:58,280 --> 00:12:59,740
which will then lead to tissue damage.

185
00:13:00,200 --> 00:13:03,100
This disease presents in childhood and manifests as cirrhosis.

186
00:13:03,640 --> 00:13:05,820
Patients can also have neurologic manifestations.

187
00:13:05,820 --> 00:13:09,820
Now among the neurologic manifestations includes behavioral changes,

188
00:13:10,260 --> 00:13:14,600
dementia, chorea, Parkinson-like symptoms, etc.

189
00:13:14,860 --> 00:13:18,240
And so this can mimic many of the other disorders that we'll study

190
00:13:18,240 --> 00:13:20,440
when we learn about neurodegenerative disease.

191
00:13:20,740 --> 00:13:23,520
So for example, if a child has Parkinson's-like symptoms,

192
00:13:23,600 --> 00:13:26,120
we might think about Wilson's disease.

193
00:13:26,460 --> 00:13:28,920
Similarly, dementia or chorea,

194
00:13:29,240 --> 00:13:32,780
which you might normally associate with Huntington's disease, for example,

195
00:13:32,780 --> 00:13:36,040
you might want to think about Wilson's disease as another possibility.

196
00:13:36,740 --> 00:13:39,080
There's also deposition of copper within the cornea,

197
00:13:39,200 --> 00:13:40,740
resulting in Kaiser-Flesher rings.

198
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:44,380
Of course, because there's going to be a generation of free radicals by the copper,

199
00:13:44,740 --> 00:13:47,060
there'll be an increased risk of hepatocellular carcinoma.

200
00:13:47,460 --> 00:13:49,020
The treatment here is D-penicillamine,

201
00:13:49,360 --> 00:13:51,480
which is basically a copper chelating agent.

202
00:13:52,040 --> 00:13:54,820
Now on laboratory exam, there will be an increased urinary copper.

203
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,420
Some of the copper in the serum will actually leak out into the urine.

204
00:13:57,820 --> 00:13:59,780
There'll be decreased serum ceruloplasmin.

205
00:13:59,780 --> 00:14:02,780
Again, we don't have the ability to put copper into ceruloplasmin,

206
00:14:03,560 --> 00:14:07,260
and so therefore, we won't have as much ceruloplasmin floating around within the blood.

207
00:14:07,680 --> 00:14:10,000
And finally, there'll be increased copper on liver biopsy

208
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,080
because copper can't be put into the bile.

209
00:14:12,720 --> 00:14:15,400
Primary biliary cirrhosis is an autoimmune disease

210
00:14:15,400 --> 00:14:19,000
in which you get granulominus destruction of the intrahepatic bile ducts.

211
00:14:19,380 --> 00:14:20,680
This is classically seen in women.

212
00:14:20,800 --> 00:14:21,760
The average age is 40,

213
00:14:22,100 --> 00:14:24,560
and it can be associated with other autoimmune diseases.

214
00:14:24,860 --> 00:14:25,820
But that's nothing special

215
00:14:25,820 --> 00:14:30,160
because almost every autoimmune disease can be associated with other autoimmune diseases.

216
00:14:30,640 --> 00:14:31,720
We don't know the etiology,

217
00:14:31,880 --> 00:14:35,680
but we do know that there's an antimitylchondrial antibody that's present in the serum,

218
00:14:36,040 --> 00:14:37,400
and that helps to mark the disease.

219
00:14:37,700 --> 00:14:40,660
And that's very high yield for the purposes of examinations.

220
00:14:41,140 --> 00:14:42,980
The patients present with obstructive jaundice,

221
00:14:43,020 --> 00:14:45,320
and that's because you're knocking out the bile ducts.

222
00:14:45,440 --> 00:14:47,180
And cirrhosis is a late complication.

223
00:14:47,600 --> 00:14:49,380
With all that obstruction,

224
00:14:49,800 --> 00:14:51,080
eventually you get liver damage,

225
00:14:51,080 --> 00:14:52,820
which can then lead to cirrhosis,

226
00:14:53,260 --> 00:14:55,680
and hence the name primary biliary cirrhosis.

227
00:14:56,640 --> 00:14:59,240
Primary sclerosing cholangitis is a disorder

228
00:14:59,240 --> 00:15:01,120
in which you get inflammation and fibrosis

229
00:15:01,120 --> 00:15:04,420
of the intrahepatic and extrahepatic bile ducts.

230
00:15:04,920 --> 00:15:07,020
This results in perioductal fibrosis

231
00:15:07,020 --> 00:15:09,800
with a classic onion skin appearance.

232
00:15:10,360 --> 00:15:12,620
Here's an image highlighting the perioductal fibrosis.

233
00:15:12,940 --> 00:15:14,620
Notice that you've got all this fibrosis

234
00:15:14,620 --> 00:15:16,420
wrapping around this bile duct,

235
00:15:16,760 --> 00:15:18,940
and you can clearly see it's associated with inflammation.

236
00:15:18,940 --> 00:15:22,160
And so the characteristic is perioductal fibrosis

237
00:15:22,160 --> 00:15:23,620
with an onion skin appearance.

238
00:15:23,960 --> 00:15:25,860
What you would see if this was the duct

239
00:15:25,860 --> 00:15:27,600
is that you would have fibrosis,

240
00:15:28,020 --> 00:15:29,420
and then fibrosis here,

241
00:15:29,820 --> 00:15:31,080
and then fibrosis here,

242
00:15:31,240 --> 00:15:33,940
and the regions in between would appear slightly dilated.

243
00:15:34,320 --> 00:15:35,400
And so what this creates

244
00:15:35,400 --> 00:15:37,200
is sort of a string of pearl appearance,

245
00:15:37,300 --> 00:15:38,160
where this is a pearl,

246
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:40,180
and this is the string in between the pearl,

247
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:41,680
and this is again the pearl,

248
00:15:42,260 --> 00:15:44,660
with the fibrosed areas representing the string

249
00:15:44,660 --> 00:15:46,000
in between the pearl.

250
00:15:46,500 --> 00:15:48,300
The etiology of this disorder is unknown,

251
00:15:48,300 --> 00:15:50,320
but it's very high yield to note,

252
00:15:50,460 --> 00:15:51,520
especially for board exams,

253
00:15:51,840 --> 00:15:53,780
that it's associated with ulcerative colitis,

254
00:15:54,180 --> 00:15:56,400
and also that the P anca can be positive.

255
00:15:56,700 --> 00:15:57,740
But you're well aware of the fact

256
00:15:57,740 --> 00:15:59,200
that patients with ulcerative colitis

257
00:15:59,200 --> 00:16:00,580
can also have a P anca,

258
00:16:00,740 --> 00:16:02,540
so that shouldn't be too hard to remember.

259
00:16:03,320 --> 00:16:05,540
Patients classically present with obstructive jaundice,

260
00:16:05,600 --> 00:16:07,760
and again, that's because they're blocking the bile duct,

261
00:16:07,820 --> 00:16:09,620
and so they're going to get the classic signs and symptoms

262
00:16:09,620 --> 00:16:10,920
that we've discussed previously.

263
00:16:11,560 --> 00:16:13,180
Cirrhosis is a late complication,

264
00:16:13,440 --> 00:16:16,180
and there's an increased risk for cholangiocarcinoma,

265
00:16:16,180 --> 00:16:17,900
which is cancer of the bile ducts.

266
00:16:18,620 --> 00:16:20,400
Rice syndrome is fulminant liver failure

267
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:21,900
and encephalopathy in a child

268
00:16:21,900 --> 00:16:24,320
who has a viral illness and then takes aspirin.

269
00:16:24,760 --> 00:16:25,880
We don't know what causes it,

270
00:16:25,960 --> 00:16:27,460
but we do know of the association.

271
00:16:27,880 --> 00:16:29,800
In fact, if you purchase any bottle of aspirin

272
00:16:29,800 --> 00:16:30,980
and read the back label,

273
00:16:31,300 --> 00:16:33,120
it'll clearly say do not give aspirin

274
00:16:33,120 --> 00:16:34,620
to a child with a viral illness.

275
00:16:35,140 --> 00:16:37,640
We don't fully understand the mechanism of this disorder.

276
00:16:37,980 --> 00:16:40,420
However, we do know that there is mitochondrial damage

277
00:16:40,420 --> 00:16:41,400
of the hepatocytes,

278
00:16:41,480 --> 00:16:43,080
and perhaps that plays an important role.

279
00:16:43,080 --> 00:16:46,100
Again, high yield to know that there is mitochondrial damage

280
00:16:46,100 --> 00:16:47,080
of the hepatocytes.

281
00:16:47,600 --> 00:16:49,500
These patients present with hypoglycemia,

282
00:16:49,840 --> 00:16:51,460
markedly elevated liver enzymes,

283
00:16:51,820 --> 00:16:52,840
and nausea with vomiting,

284
00:16:53,180 --> 00:16:54,920
and it can progress to coma and death.

285
00:16:55,240 --> 00:16:56,300
Now, I should make a note here.

286
00:16:56,380 --> 00:16:57,820
You generally never give a child

287
00:16:57,820 --> 00:16:59,180
with a viral illness aspirin.

288
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:01,440
However, remember that there is one disease

289
00:17:01,440 --> 00:17:03,240
that can sort of look like a viral illness,

290
00:17:03,460 --> 00:17:05,560
but would require that you give the patient aspirin.

291
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:07,220
Do you recall that disease?

292
00:17:07,860 --> 00:17:09,280
The answer is Kawasaki syndrome,

293
00:17:09,280 --> 00:17:11,740
which is a vasculitis that preferentially involves

294
00:17:11,740 --> 00:17:14,620
the coronary arteries and presents with signs and symptoms

295
00:17:14,620 --> 00:17:17,560
like conjunctivitis and fever, etc.,

296
00:17:17,560 --> 00:17:18,820
that mimic a viral illness.

297
00:17:19,280 --> 00:17:21,440
And again, that's high yield for board examinations.

298
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:23,160
Moving on to liver tumors,

299
00:17:23,300 --> 00:17:25,900
hepatic adenoma is a benign tumor of hepatocytes.

300
00:17:26,460 --> 00:17:27,620
Very high yield association

301
00:17:27,620 --> 00:17:30,480
is that it is related to oral contraceptive use

302
00:17:30,480 --> 00:17:32,960
and that it will regress upon cessation

303
00:17:32,960 --> 00:17:34,540
of the oral contraceptives.

304
00:17:34,860 --> 00:17:36,380
Now, although this is a benign tumor,

305
00:17:36,380 --> 00:17:39,220
there is a feared complication that it can rupture,

306
00:17:39,560 --> 00:17:41,480
resulting in intraperitoneal hemorrhage,

307
00:17:41,760 --> 00:17:42,960
especially during pregnancy

308
00:17:42,960 --> 00:17:44,460
when there's high levels of estrogen,

309
00:17:44,780 --> 00:17:46,360
which can drive the growth of this tumor.

310
00:17:46,760 --> 00:17:48,680
Now, again, the reason why there's a risk of rupture

311
00:17:48,680 --> 00:17:50,640
is because the tumors are subcaptular,

312
00:17:50,760 --> 00:17:52,320
right underneath the capsule of the liver,

313
00:17:52,660 --> 00:17:54,180
and so they can rupture through the capsule

314
00:17:54,180 --> 00:17:56,340
and then cause intraperitoneal hemorrhage.

315
00:17:56,860 --> 00:17:58,900
The risk factors for hepatocellular carcinoma

316
00:17:58,900 --> 00:18:00,180
include chronic hepatitis.

317
00:18:00,260 --> 00:18:03,060
So, for example, hepatitis B virus and hepatitis C virus,

318
00:18:03,180 --> 00:18:05,520
both of which can result in chronic hepatitis,

319
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:07,920
can increase the risk for hepatocellular carcinoma.

320
00:18:08,620 --> 00:18:10,140
In addition, any cause of cirrhosis

321
00:18:10,140 --> 00:18:12,480
is a risk factor for hepatocellular carcinoma.

322
00:18:12,880 --> 00:18:15,760
For example, non-alcoholic fatty liver disease,

323
00:18:16,180 --> 00:18:18,060
hemochromatosis, Wilson's disease,

324
00:18:18,280 --> 00:18:20,360
alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, etc.

325
00:18:20,560 --> 00:18:22,660
All of these are important causes of cirrhosis.

326
00:18:23,200 --> 00:18:25,340
Finally, one super high yield risk factor

327
00:18:25,340 --> 00:18:27,520
is aflatoxins derived from aspergillus.

328
00:18:27,980 --> 00:18:30,720
Recall from our lecture in the neoplasia chapter

329
00:18:30,720 --> 00:18:33,040
that I told you that in some countries of the world,

330
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:36,000
there is a very high incidence of hepatocellular carcinoma

331
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:37,340
due to this mechanism.

332
00:18:37,760 --> 00:18:38,560
Now, in those countries,

333
00:18:38,660 --> 00:18:40,800
they store grains for extended periods of time,

334
00:18:41,140 --> 00:18:43,300
and aspergillus tends to grow on those grains.

335
00:18:43,640 --> 00:18:45,280
It then releases aflatoxins,

336
00:18:45,360 --> 00:18:46,320
which when consumed,

337
00:18:46,620 --> 00:18:49,320
result in an increased risk for hepatocellular carcinoma.

338
00:18:49,700 --> 00:18:51,860
Very high yield for board examinations.

339
00:18:52,280 --> 00:18:54,820
The aflatoxins actually induce P53 mutations,

340
00:18:55,240 --> 00:18:56,240
and that's the mechanism

341
00:18:56,240 --> 00:18:58,580
by which hepatocellular carcinoma will arise.

342
00:18:59,180 --> 00:19:01,960
Hepatocellular carcinoma loves to invade the hepatic vein,

343
00:19:01,960 --> 00:19:03,980
and any time you invade the hepatic vein,

344
00:19:04,060 --> 00:19:05,560
that increases the risk for a syndrome

345
00:19:05,560 --> 00:19:06,800
called Bud Chiari syndrome.

346
00:19:07,180 --> 00:19:09,620
The idea here is that if this is the liver,

347
00:19:10,800 --> 00:19:13,400
recall that you've got a portal vein going into the liver,

348
00:19:13,640 --> 00:19:15,920
and then you've got a hepatic vein coming out of the liver.

349
00:19:16,520 --> 00:19:17,580
In Bud Chiari syndrome,

350
00:19:17,720 --> 00:19:19,440
you get thrombosis of the hepatic vein,

351
00:19:19,760 --> 00:19:22,400
which can then result in painful hepatosplenomegaly,

352
00:19:22,720 --> 00:19:23,160
ascites,

353
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:25,900
and eventual necrosis of the liver with death.

354
00:19:26,320 --> 00:19:28,420
Now, among the causes of Bud Chiari,

355
00:19:28,820 --> 00:19:31,040
one is hepatocellular carcinoma,

356
00:19:31,040 --> 00:19:33,600
and that's because if hepatocellular carcinoma

357
00:19:33,600 --> 00:19:34,600
is growing within the liver,

358
00:19:35,140 --> 00:19:37,340
it has a propensity to involve the hepatic vein,

359
00:19:37,540 --> 00:19:39,180
which could then result in thrombosis.

360
00:19:39,740 --> 00:19:40,760
Of course, this would present

361
00:19:40,760 --> 00:19:42,560
with painful hepatomegaly and ascites

362
00:19:42,560 --> 00:19:44,580
and could potentially lead to death.

363
00:19:45,340 --> 00:19:47,500
Hepatocellular carcinoma has a poor prognosis,

364
00:19:47,620 --> 00:19:49,780
and that's because the tumors are often detected late,

365
00:19:50,080 --> 00:19:52,120
and that's because usually the patients have cirrhosis

366
00:19:52,120 --> 00:19:53,780
or some other problem in the liver,

367
00:19:54,080 --> 00:19:55,780
and that masks the symptoms

368
00:19:55,780 --> 00:19:57,600
of the hepatocellular carcinoma.

369
00:19:57,600 --> 00:20:00,060
One of the serum tumor markers that is useful

370
00:20:00,060 --> 00:20:02,160
for hepatocellular carcinoma is alpha-fetoprotein.

371
00:20:03,160 --> 00:20:04,200
That's important to remember.

372
00:20:04,700 --> 00:20:06,880
The final point I'd like to make concerning liver tumors

373
00:20:06,880 --> 00:20:08,260
is metastasis to the liver.

374
00:20:08,820 --> 00:20:11,380
Metastasis to the liver is more common than primary tumors.

375
00:20:11,940 --> 00:20:14,360
Important sources include colon carcinoma,

376
00:20:14,500 --> 00:20:15,640
pancreatic carcinoma,

377
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,400
and then lung and breast can also spread to the liver.

378
00:20:18,900 --> 00:20:19,920
When you have metastasis,

379
00:20:19,920 --> 00:20:21,900
you're going to get multiple nodules in the liver,

380
00:20:22,260 --> 00:20:24,020
and clinically, this is going to be detected

381
00:20:24,020 --> 00:20:26,120
as a large liver or hepatomegaly

382
00:20:26,120 --> 00:20:28,240
with a nodular free edge of the liver.

383
00:20:28,360 --> 00:20:30,520
When you feel the edge of the liver on physical exam,

384
00:20:30,620 --> 00:20:32,180
you can feel the bumps in the liver

385
00:20:32,180 --> 00:20:34,580
because there are multiple nodules within the liver.

386
00:20:35,160 --> 00:20:36,840
Here's a classic image just highlighting

387
00:20:36,840 --> 00:20:39,420
that you've got multiple nodules within the liver,

388
00:20:39,580 --> 00:20:41,800
and of course, we would feel those nodules

389
00:20:41,800 --> 00:20:44,320
on the free edge of the liver on physical exam.

390
00:20:44,740 --> 00:20:47,340
And that concludes this section as well as the chapter.

